-- Q1: easy / L1 — classification of accounts receivable as monetary item
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$For purposes of foreign currency translation under ASC 830, how is an accounts receivable denominated in a foreign currency classified?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["As a nonmonetary asset translated at the historical rate", "As a monetary asset translated at the current exchange rate", "As a nonmonetary asset translated at the weighted-average rate", "As an equity item excluded from translation adjustments"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 830-10-45, accounts receivable is a monetary asset — it represents a right to receive a fixed or determinable number of currency units. Monetary items are translated at the current exchange rate on the balance sheet date under both the current rate method and the temporal method. Wrong (A): Accounts receivable is monetary, not nonmonetary; nonmonetary items include inventory at cost and fixed assets. Wrong (C): The weighted-average rate is used for income statement items, not balance sheet monetary assets. Wrong (D): Accounts receivable is an asset, not an equity item, and is always subject to translation.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — mark-to-market approach for excluded forward points
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Greystone Corp. (U.S.) designates a forward contract as a cash flow hedge and excludes forward points from the effectiveness assessment. Under ASC 815-20-25, Greystone elects the mark-to-market approach (rather than amortization) for the excluded component. At the reporting date, the fair value change attributable to forward points is $18,000. How is this amount recognized?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Deferred in OCI until the hedged transaction occurs", "Recognized immediately in earnings in the current period", "Amortized to earnings ratably over the remaining hedge term", "Netted against the effective portion of the hedge in OCI"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 815-20-25-83A, when an entity elects the mark-to-market approach for an excluded component (such as forward points), the entire fair value change attributable to the excluded component is recognized in current-period earnings. This contrasts with the amortization approach, which spreads the initial value systematically. Wrong (A): OCI treatment applies to the effective hedge portion (intrinsic/spot changes), not to an excluded component under the mark-to-market approach. Wrong (C): Systematic amortization is the alternative approach; the mark-to-market method recognizes changes immediately. Wrong (D): Excluded components are reported separately from the effective hedge portion and are not netted.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — evaluating embedded FC derivative bifurcation
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Meridian Corp. (U.S., USD functional currency) enters a five-year supply contract with a vendor in Japan. The contract requires payment in Brazilian reais (BRL), which is neither party's functional currency nor the currency customarily used for such transactions in the economic environment. Under ASC 815-15, how should the embedded foreign currency feature be evaluated?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No bifurcation is needed because all executory contracts are exempt from derivative accounting", "The embedded currency feature must be bifurcated and accounted for as a separate derivative because BRL is not clearly and closely related to either party's economic environment", "The embedded feature is clearly and closely related because the vendor operates in an emerging market", "Bifurcation is required only if the contract's notional exceeds a materiality threshold"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 815-15-25, an embedded foreign currency derivative in an executory contract requires bifurcation when the payment currency is not the functional currency of either party and is not the currency routinely used for such transactions in the relevant economic environment. BRL meets neither criterion, so it is not clearly and closely related, requiring bifurcation. Wrong (A): Executory contracts are not categorically exempt from embedded derivative analysis under ASC 815. Wrong (C): The vendor's location in an emerging market does not make a third-party currency clearly and closely related. Wrong (D): ASC 815 does not apply a notional threshold for embedded derivative bifurcation.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — remeasurement of marketable equity securities under temporal method
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Henley Inc. (U.S.) has a foreign subsidiary whose functional currency is USD. The subsidiary holds marketable equity securities carried at fair value of LC 600,000 on the balance sheet date. The securities were purchased when the rate was $0.85/LC, and the year-end rate is $0.80/LC. Under the temporal method in ASC 830, at what rate are these securities remeasured?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The historical rate of $0.85/LC when the securities were purchased", "The year-end current rate of $0.80/LC because they are carried at fair value", "The weighted-average rate for the period", "The lower of the historical or current rate"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 830-10-55, the temporal method remeasures assets at the rate consistent with how they are measured. Assets carried at current fair value (such as marketable equity securities at fair value) are remeasured at the current exchange rate, not the historical rate. LC 600,000 x $0.80 = $480,000. Wrong (A): The historical rate applies to assets carried at historical cost, not at fair value. Wrong (C): Weighted-average rates apply to income statement items, not balance sheet fair-value assets. Wrong (D): There is no lower-of-cost-or-market concept for exchange rate selection under ASC 830.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — where remeasurement gains/losses are reported
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$When a foreign subsidiary's functional currency is the U.S. dollar and its books are maintained in the local currency, where are the remeasurement gains and losses reported under ASC 830?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["In other comprehensive income as a cumulative translation adjustment", "In the income statement as part of earnings", "As a direct adjustment to retained earnings", "As a separate component of stockholders' equity outside of OCI"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 830-10-45, when the functional currency is the reporting currency (USD) and the books are in a different local currency, the temporal method is used. Remeasurement gains and losses under the temporal method are recognized in earnings on the income statement. Wrong (A): CTA in OCI arises from translation under the current rate method, not from remeasurement under the temporal method. Wrong (C): Remeasurement gains/losses flow through the income statement, not directly to retained earnings. Wrong (D): There is no separate equity component for remeasurement results; they are included in earnings.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — analyzing earnings impact based on net monetary position direction
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Oakridge Corp. (U.S.) has a subsidiary that uses the temporal method because its functional currency is USD. The subsidiary holds net monetary liabilities of LC 4,000,000 (monetary liabilities exceed monetary assets). During the period, the local currency depreciated 12% against the dollar. What is the expected earnings effect of remeasurement?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A remeasurement loss because the subsidiary's liabilities cost more in dollars", "A remeasurement gain because the net monetary liabilities decrease in dollar terms as the local currency weakens", "No remeasurement effect because monetary assets and liabilities offset each other", "A remeasurement loss recognized in OCI rather than earnings"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the temporal method, net monetary liabilities are exposed to exchange rate changes. When the local currency depreciates, net monetary liabilities translate into fewer dollars, producing a remeasurement gain. The entity effectively owes less in dollar terms. Wrong (A): A weakening local currency reduces the dollar value of liabilities, creating a gain, not a loss, on a net monetary liability position. Wrong (C): When monetary liabilities exceed monetary assets, there is a net exposed position that produces gains or losses. Wrong (D): Remeasurement gains and losses under the temporal method are recognized in earnings, not OCI.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — disclosure requirement for aggregate transaction gains/losses
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Under ASC 830-20-50, what must an entity disclose in its financial statements regarding foreign currency transactions?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Only the net amount of transaction gains or losses included in net income", "The aggregate transaction gain or loss included in determining net income for the period", "A detailed schedule of each individual foreign currency transaction during the period", "Only transaction losses exceeding 5% of net income before taxes"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 830-20-50-1, an entity must disclose the aggregate transaction gain or loss included in determining net income for the period. This disclosure informs users about the earnings impact of exchange rate changes on the entity's foreign currency transactions. Wrong (A): While the net amount is part of the aggregate, the standard specifically requires disclosure of the aggregate amount, not merely the net. Wrong (C): ASC 830 does not require a transaction-by-transaction schedule — aggregate disclosure is sufficient. Wrong (D): There is no materiality threshold of 5% in ASC 830-20-50; the aggregate must be disclosed regardless of amount.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — analyzing temporal method result with LIFO inventory
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Whitmore Industries (U.S.) has a subsidiary whose functional currency is USD. The subsidiary uses LIFO for inventory costing. The local currency steadily appreciated against the dollar throughout the year. Under the temporal method, how does the LIFO assumption affect the dollar amount of cost of goods sold compared to FIFO?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["LIFO COGS is higher in dollars because it uses the most recently purchased (lower historical dollar rate) inventory", "LIFO COGS is lower in dollars because the most recent purchases were made when the local currency was stronger, translating at higher dollar rates", "LIFO COGS equals FIFO COGS because both use the weighted-average rate for expense translation", "LIFO COGS is higher in dollars because the most recent purchases translate at higher dollar rates due to the appreciating local currency"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 830-10-55, COGS is remeasured at the historical rates when inventory was purchased. With LIFO, the most recent purchases are sold first. Since the local currency appreciated steadily, the most recent inventory was acquired at stronger local currency rates (higher dollar equivalents), making LIFO COGS higher in dollars than FIFO COGS. Wrong (A): The local currency appreciated, so more recent rates are higher in dollar terms, not lower. Wrong (B): Higher dollar rates on recent purchases make LIFO COGS higher, not lower. Wrong (C): The temporal method uses specific historical rates for inventory, not the weighted-average rate.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: easy / L2 — computing exchange loss on foreign currency payable
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Bridger Co. (U.S.) purchases equipment from a German vendor for EUR 400,000 on June 1 when the spot rate is $1.10/EUR. At the June 30 interim reporting date, the spot rate is $1.14/EUR. The payable remains unsettled. What exchange gain or loss does Bridger report at June 30?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$16,000 transaction gain", "$16,000 transaction loss", "$456,000 transaction loss", "$440,000 transaction gain"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 830-20-35, Bridger remeasures the EUR payable at the interim date. The payable increased from EUR 400,000 x $1.10 = $440,000 to EUR 400,000 x $1.14 = $456,000, a $16,000 increase in the dollar obligation. Because Bridger owes more dollars, this is a $16,000 transaction loss. Wrong (A): The euro strengthened, increasing the payable — this creates a loss, not a gain, on a liability denominated in the foreign currency. Wrong (C): $456,000 is the remeasured payable balance, not the loss amount. Wrong (D): $440,000 is the original recorded amount, not a gain.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — analyzing functional currency indicators when they conflict
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Calloway Corp. (U.S.) has a subsidiary in Singapore. The subsidiary generates 80% of its revenue in Singapore dollars (SGD), incurs 70% of its operating costs in SGD, and its day-to-day operations are managed locally. However, the subsidiary's financing is entirely in USD, and its parent provides all capital funding. Under ASC 830-10-55, which set of indicators most likely determines the functional currency?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The financing indicators dominate, making USD the functional currency", "The cash flow and expense indicators dominate, making SGD the functional currency because the subsidiary primarily generates and expends cash in SGD", "The subsidiary must use the parent's reporting currency whenever any indicator points to USD", "Management may freely choose either currency when indicators conflict"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 830-10-55, management must weigh all functional currency indicators, with cash flow and operating indicators generally given significant weight. When revenue, expenses, and day-to-day management are predominantly in SGD, the cash flow indicators point strongly to SGD despite USD financing. The entity primarily generates and expends cash in SGD. Wrong (A): Financing is one indicator but does not automatically override operating cash flow indicators. Wrong (C): ASC 830 does not require use of the parent's currency when only some indicators point to USD. Wrong (D): Functional currency determination is based on economic facts, not management's free choice; the indicators must be weighed objectively.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: easy / L2 — computing dollar proceeds from settling a forward contract
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Tidewater Corp. (U.S.) entered a 30-day forward contract to sell GBP 500,000 at a forward rate of $1.26/GBP. At settlement, the spot rate is $1.22/GBP. What is the net cash flow to Tidewater from settling this forward contract?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$20,000 cash inflow (gain on the forward)", "$20,000 cash outflow (loss on the forward)", "$610,000 total cash received", "$630,000 total cash received"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Tidewater contracted to sell GBP 500,000 at $1.26/GBP, receiving $630,000. The market rate at settlement is $1.22/GBP, so the GBP is worth only $610,000 at spot. The net settlement gain is GBP 500,000 x ($1.26 - $1.22) = $20,000 cash inflow to Tidewater because it sold GBP at an above-market rate. Wrong (B): Tidewater sold GBP at a rate higher than the spot rate, so it gains, not loses. Wrong (C): $610,000 is the market value of the GBP, not the net settlement on the forward. Wrong (D): $630,000 is the gross contract value, not the net settlement amount.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q12: hard / L4 — evaluating hedge accounting when critical terms do not match
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Wexford Inc. (U.S.) designates a EUR forward contract as a cash flow hedge of a forecasted EUR-denominated inventory purchase. The forward contract matures on June 30, but the forecasted purchase is expected on July 15. Under ASC 815-20-25, which factor is most critical in determining whether the hedge qualifies for the shortcut method or requires quantitative effectiveness testing?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The 15-day maturity mismatch prevents any form of hedge accounting", "The shortcut method is available because the underlying exposure and the derivative reference the same currency", "Critical terms do not perfectly match due to the maturity date difference, so the shortcut method is unavailable and quantitative effectiveness testing is required", "The shortcut method is available if management documents that the mismatch is immaterial"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 815-20-25, the shortcut method for foreign currency hedges requires that critical terms of the derivative perfectly match the hedged item, including notional amount, currency, and timing. A 15-day maturity mismatch means critical terms do not align, disqualifying the shortcut method. The entity must use quantitative testing (e.g., regression or dollar-offset) to demonstrate effectiveness. Wrong (A): A maturity mismatch does not prevent hedge accounting entirely — it only prevents use of the shortcut method. Wrong (B): Matching currencies alone is insufficient; timing and notional must also match for the shortcut method. Wrong (D): The shortcut method has strict criteria and does not allow materiality exceptions for term mismatches.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q13: easy / L2 — computing the dollar cost when settlement rate differs
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Lakeview Corp. (U.S.) purchases raw materials from a Mexican supplier for MXN 5,000,000 on November 1 when the spot rate is $0.058/MXN. Payment is made on December 1 when the spot rate is $0.055/MXN. What is the total dollar amount Lakeview pays at settlement, and what transaction gain or loss is recognized?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Pays $290,000 with no gain or loss", "Pays $275,000 with a $15,000 transaction gain", "Pays $290,000 with a $15,000 transaction loss", "Pays $275,000 with a $15,000 transaction loss"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Lakeview recorded the payable at MXN 5,000,000 x $0.058 = $290,000. At settlement, it pays MXN 5,000,000 x $0.055 = $275,000. The peso weakened, so the payable costs $15,000 less to settle, producing a $15,000 transaction gain. Wrong (A): The payable is settled at the spot rate on the payment date ($0.055), not the original rate. Wrong (C): Lakeview pays $275,000 at settlement, not $290,000; the decrease creates a gain. Wrong (D): The peso weakened, reducing the dollar cost — this is a gain on a payable, not a loss.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — accounting for natural hedge through matched FC positions
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Crawford Industries (U.S.) has a EUR 3,000,000 receivable from a European customer and a separate EUR 3,000,000 payable to a European vendor, both due in 60 days. The CFO asks whether a formal hedge is necessary. Under ASC 830, what is the net foreign currency exposure on these positions, and how are they reported?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Zero net exposure; the receivable and payable may be netted and no transaction gains or losses are recognized", "Zero net exposure on a combined basis, but each position must be remeasured separately, with offsetting gains and losses reported individually in earnings", "EUR 6,000,000 gross exposure; both positions must be hedged separately", "EUR 3,000,000 net exposure; only the receivable creates foreign currency risk"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 830-20-35, each foreign-currency-denominated monetary item must be remeasured individually at the current rate. While the EUR receivable and payable create offsetting economic exposures (a natural hedge), the gains and losses on each are recognized separately in earnings. They naturally offset, but ASC 830 does not permit netting for measurement purposes. Wrong (A): Each item must be remeasured independently; the standard does not allow netting to skip recognition. Wrong (C): While the gross notional is EUR 6,000,000, the economic exposure nets to approximately zero — no separate hedging is typically needed. Wrong (D): Both the receivable and payable create foreign currency risk; the payable is also exposed.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — analyzing goodwill translation from a foreign acquisition
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Sterling Corp. (U.S.) acquired a foreign subsidiary for its local currency (LC). Goodwill of LC 8,000,000 was recognized at acquisition when the exchange rate was $0.75/LC. At the current balance sheet date, the rate is $0.70/LC. Under ASC 830-10-45, how is the goodwill translated, and what is the financial statement effect?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Goodwill remains at $6,000,000 using the historical acquisition-date rate with no CTA effect", "Goodwill is translated at $5,600,000 using the current rate, with the $400,000 decrease included in the CTA in OCI", "Goodwill is tested for impairment in dollars, then translated at the current rate", "Goodwill is translated at the weighted-average rate, producing a $5,800,000 balance"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 830-10-45-17, goodwill arising from the acquisition of a foreign entity with a local currency functional currency is translated at the current exchange rate, the same as other assets under the current rate method. LC 8,000,000 x $0.70 = $5,600,000, compared to the prior translated amount of $6,000,000. The $400,000 decrease flows to the CTA in OCI. Wrong (A): Goodwill is not held at the historical rate under the current rate method; it is retranslated each period at the current rate. Wrong (C): Impairment testing is performed in the functional currency, then translated; the translation itself does not depend on impairment testing. Wrong (D): The weighted-average rate applies to income statement items, not balance sheet assets like goodwill.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — analyzing the translation of a subsidiary's OCI items
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Burke Holdings (U.S.) has a Japanese subsidiary with JPY functional currency. The subsidiary reports an unrealized holding gain on available-for-sale debt securities of JPY 50,000,000 in its own OCI. When Burke prepares consolidated financial statements using the current rate method, how is this OCI item translated and where does the translation difference appear?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The OCI item is translated at the historical rate with no additional translation adjustment", "The OCI item is translated at the weighted-average rate and any translation difference is included in earnings", "The OCI item is translated at the current rate, and the translation difference on this item is included in the cumulative translation adjustment within OCI", "The OCI item is excluded from translation because it is already in OCI"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 830-10-45, all components of equity — including accumulated OCI items of the subsidiary — are subject to translation. The subsidiary's OCI item is translated and the resulting translation difference flows into the CTA component of the parent's consolidated OCI. Wrong (A): Under the current rate method, OCI items are not translated at historical rates. Wrong (B): Translation differences on balance sheet items go to CTA in OCI, not to earnings, under the current rate method. Wrong (D): No item is exempt from translation; all subsidiary financial statement components must be translated for consolidation.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — analyzing the effect of highly inflationary re-designation on prior CTA
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Delta Worldwide (U.S.) has a subsidiary in Country Y that previously used the local peso as its functional currency. Country Y's cumulative inflation has now exceeded 100% over three years, triggering highly inflationary status. The subsidiary has an accumulated positive CTA of $350,000 in AOCI from prior-year translations. Under ASC 830-10-45, what happens to the existing CTA balance when the subsidiary transitions to the temporal method?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The $350,000 CTA is immediately reclassified from AOCI to earnings", "The $350,000 CTA is reversed against the subsidiary's retained earnings", "The $350,000 CTA remains in AOCI; it is not reclassified merely because of the change to the temporal method", "The $350,000 CTA is netted against future remeasurement gains or losses"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 830-10-45, when a subsidiary transitions to the temporal method due to highly inflationary status, the translated amounts at the end of the prior period become the new accounting basis for nonmonetary items. The existing CTA balance remains in AOCI — it is only reclassified to earnings upon sale or complete liquidation of the foreign entity, not merely upon a change in translation method. Wrong (A): CTA reclassification to earnings requires disposal or loss of control, not a method change. Wrong (B): CTA is not reversed against retained earnings; it stays in AOCI. Wrong (D): CTA is a separate equity component and is not netted against future remeasurement results.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — what cumulative inflation threshold triggers highly inflationary status
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Under ASC 830-10-45, what cumulative inflation rate over the most recent three-year period triggers highly inflationary accounting treatment for a foreign subsidiary?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["50% or more", "75% or more", "100% or more", "200% or more"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 830-10-45-11, a highly inflationary economy is one with cumulative inflation of approximately 100% or more over a three-year period. When triggered, the subsidiary's financial statements must be remeasured as if the reporting currency (typically USD) were the functional currency. Wrong (A): 50% is below the threshold established by ASC 830. Wrong (B): 75% is below the threshold. Wrong (D): 200% far exceeds the threshold; the standard uses approximately 100%.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: medium / L2 — multi-period transaction gain/loss with interim remeasurement
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Summit Corp. (U.S.) records a GBP 1,000,000 receivable on November 1 when the spot rate is $1.30/GBP. At December 31 (fiscal year-end), the spot rate is $1.27/GBP. The receivable is collected on February 15 of the next year when the rate is $1.25/GBP. What transaction gain or loss does Summit recognize in the second fiscal year (the year of collection)?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$50,000 loss (computed from the original rate to the collection rate)", "$30,000 loss (computed from the original rate to the year-end rate)", "$20,000 loss (computed from the year-end rate to the collection rate)", "$20,000 gain (computed from the collection rate to the year-end rate)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 830-20-35, the receivable was remeasured to $1,270,000 at the prior year-end. In the second year, it is measured at collection: GBP 1,000,000 x $1.25 = $1,250,000. The loss in Year 2 is $1,270,000 - $1,250,000 = $20,000. The $30,000 loss from the original rate to year-end was recognized in Year 1. Wrong (A): The $50,000 total loss is split across two periods; only $20,000 belongs to Year 2. Wrong (B): The $30,000 loss was recognized in Year 1, not Year 2. Wrong (D): The pound weakened further from year-end to collection, creating an additional loss, not a gain.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — determining translation method based on functional currency
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Archer Holdings (U.S.) has two foreign subsidiaries. Subsidiary A operates in Germany with EUR functional currency. Subsidiary B operates in Brazil but its functional currency has been determined to be the U.S. dollar. Which translation method does ASC 830 require for each subsidiary?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Current rate method for both subsidiaries", "Temporal method for both subsidiaries", "Current rate method for Subsidiary A (EUR functional) and temporal method for Subsidiary B (USD functional)", "Temporal method for Subsidiary A (EUR functional) and current rate method for Subsidiary B (USD functional)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 830-10-45, when the functional currency is the local currency (Sub A, EUR), the current rate method is used to translate into the reporting currency (USD). When the functional currency is the reporting currency (Sub B, USD), the temporal method is used to remeasure from the local currency into USD. Wrong (A): Subsidiary B's functional currency is USD, requiring the temporal method, not the current rate method. Wrong (B): Subsidiary A's functional currency is EUR (its local currency), requiring the current rate method. Wrong (D): This reverses the correct assignment; the current rate method applies when the local currency is functional.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — evaluating hedge accounting documentation requirements
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Falcon Corp. (U.S.) enters a forward contract to buy EUR 2,000,000 to hedge a forecasted EUR purchase. The treasury team executes the forward on March 1 but does not complete the formal hedge designation documentation until March 20. Between March 1 and March 20, the forward's fair value increased by $12,000. Under ASC 815-20-25, what is the consequence of the delayed documentation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Hedge accounting may be applied retroactively to March 1 if documentation is completed within 30 days", "Hedge accounting begins on March 20 (the documentation date), and the $12,000 fair value change from March 1 to March 20 must be recognized in earnings", "The forward contract is permanently disqualified from hedge accounting", "The $12,000 pre-designation gain is deferred in OCI as part of the hedge"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 815-20-25, hedge accounting can only begin on or after the date all designation and documentation requirements are met. Since documentation was completed on March 20, hedge accounting starts that date. The $12,000 fair value change from March 1 to March 20 occurred before designation and must be recognized in earnings as an undesignated derivative gain. Wrong (A): ASC 815 does not allow retroactive hedge designation; it must be concurrent with or prior to the hedging relationship. Wrong (C): Late documentation does not permanently disqualify the derivative — hedge accounting begins when documentation is complete. Wrong (D): Fair value changes before the hedge designation date cannot be deferred in OCI.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — which component of equity receives the CTA
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$When translating a foreign subsidiary's financial statements under the current rate method, the cumulative translation adjustment (CTA) is reported in which component of the consolidated balance sheet?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Retained earnings on the consolidated balance sheet", "Additional paid-in capital on the consolidated balance sheet", "Accumulated other comprehensive income within stockholders' equity", "A contra-asset account offsetting the investment in subsidiary"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 830-10-45, the CTA arising from translating a foreign subsidiary's financial statements under the current rate method is reported as a component of accumulated other comprehensive income (AOCI) within stockholders' equity on the consolidated balance sheet. Wrong (A): CTA does not flow through retained earnings; it bypasses net income entirely. Wrong (B): Additional paid-in capital reflects capital contributions, not translation adjustments. Wrong (D): CTA is an equity component, not a contra-asset; the investment in subsidiary is eliminated in consolidation.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: hard / L4 — evaluating intercompany loan as long-term investment nature
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Pacific Corp. (U.S.) has a JPY-denominated intercompany receivable from its Japanese subsidiary. The loan has no scheduled repayment date, and Pacific does not foresee collection in the foreseeable future. The CFO argues that exchange gains and losses on this receivable should be reported in OCI rather than earnings. Under ASC 830-20-35, is this treatment appropriate?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No, because intercompany loans always produce transaction gains or losses in earnings regardless of repayment terms", "Yes, because any receivable without a fixed maturity date qualifies for OCI treatment", "Yes, because an intercompany foreign currency transaction that is of a long-term investment nature (settlement not planned or anticipated in the foreseeable future) produces exchange adjustments reported in OCI as part of the CTA", "No, because only derivative instruments designated as net investment hedges can produce OCI adjustments for foreign currency"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 830-20-35-3, exchange gains and losses on an intercompany foreign currency transaction are reported in OCI (as part of CTA) when the transaction is of a long-term investment nature — meaning settlement is neither planned nor anticipated in the foreseeable future. Pacific's loan meets this criterion. Wrong (A): ASC 830 provides a specific exception for long-term investment nature intercompany transactions. Wrong (B): The absence of a fixed maturity alone is insufficient; the key criterion is that settlement is not planned or anticipated, requiring judgment about management's intent. Wrong (D): Both intercompany transactions of a long-term investment nature and net investment hedge instruments can produce OCI adjustments for foreign currency.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — accounting for deferred tax on CTA
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Kensington Corp. (U.S.) has a wholly owned Japanese subsidiary with JPY functional currency. The subsidiary has a positive CTA of $500,000 in AOCI. U.S. tax law would tax the CTA as a gain when the subsidiary is sold. Under ASC 740, how should the deferred tax effect on the CTA be reported?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["As a deferred tax liability recognized in income tax expense on the income statement", "As a deferred tax liability recognized in OCI, offsetting the CTA", "No deferred tax is recognized because CTA is a permanent difference", "As a deferred tax asset in OCI because the gain is not yet realized"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 740-20-45, the tax effect of items reported in OCI is also reported in OCI, not in income tax expense. Because the CTA is in AOCI and represents a temporary difference (taxable upon sale), a deferred tax liability is recognized in OCI to match the CTA. Wrong (A): Intraperiod tax allocation under ASC 740 requires the tax effect to follow the item — since CTA is in OCI, the deferred tax goes to OCI, not the income statement. Wrong (C): CTA is a temporary difference (it reverses upon sale), not a permanent difference. Wrong (D): A positive CTA represents a future taxable amount, creating a deferred tax liability, not an asset.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — analyzing income statement presentation of transaction gains/losses
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Harwood Corp. (U.S.) reports $45,000 of net foreign currency transaction losses from its EUR-denominated receivables and payables. The company's controller classified these losses as operating expenses, while the external auditor argues they belong in other income/expense. Under ASC 830-20-45, where should these transaction gains and losses be presented?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["In operating expenses because they relate to operating receivables and payables", "In other income/expense below operating income, as required by ASC 830", "Either classification is acceptable; ASC 830 does not mandate a specific income statement line item for transaction gains and losses", "In OCI as part of the cumulative translation adjustment"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 830-20-45, the aggregate transaction gain or loss included in determining net income must be disclosed, but the standard does not prescribe a specific income statement line item. Companies may present these amounts in operating expenses, other income/expense, or another caption based on their accounting policy. Wrong (A): While classification in operating expenses is permissible, it is not required. Wrong (B): Other income/expense is a common presentation, but ASC 830 does not mandate it. Wrong (D): Transaction gains and losses are recognized in earnings, not in OCI; CTA arises from translation, not from transactions.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: hard / L4 — evaluating impairment testing interaction with translation
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Riverton Group (U.S.) has a reporting unit in Canada (CAD functional currency) with goodwill of CAD 12,000,000. Riverton performs its annual goodwill impairment test. The reporting unit's fair value is estimated at CAD 50,000,000 and its carrying amount (including goodwill) is CAD 48,000,000 — no impairment in CAD. However, after translation to USD at the current rate, the translated carrying amount exceeds the translated fair value due to exchange rate changes during the period. Should Riverton recognize a goodwill impairment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, because the translated USD amounts show that carrying value exceeds fair value", "Yes, because impairment testing must be performed in the reporting currency (USD)", "No, because goodwill impairment for a foreign reporting unit is tested in the functional currency (CAD), and no impairment exists in CAD", "No, but only if the exchange rate change is deemed temporary"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 350-20-35, goodwill impairment testing for a foreign reporting unit is performed in the functional currency. Since the fair value (CAD 50,000,000) exceeds the carrying amount (CAD 48,000,000) in CAD, no impairment exists. Translation differences from converting to USD do not create impairment — they are reflected in the CTA. Wrong (A): Impairment is assessed in the functional currency, not after translation to USD. Wrong (B): ASC 350 requires testing in the functional currency of the reporting unit, not the parent's reporting currency. Wrong (D): The permanence of the exchange rate change is irrelevant; the test is conducted in the functional currency regardless.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — what creates a foreign currency transaction gain on a receivable
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$A U.S. company holds a receivable denominated in British pounds (GBP). Under ASC 830-20-35, a transaction gain on this receivable arises when which of the following occurs between the transaction date and the settlement or reporting date?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The U.S. dollar strengthens against the pound", "The pound strengthens against the U.S. dollar", "The U.S. interest rate increases relative to the U.K. rate", "The receivable is reclassified from current to long-term"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 830-20-35, a transaction gain on a foreign currency receivable arises when the foreign currency strengthens (the pound appreciates against the dollar). The receivable is worth more in dollar terms when converted at the higher rate. Wrong (A): A stronger dollar would reduce the receivable's dollar value, creating a loss, not a gain. Wrong (C): Interest rate differentials do not directly create transaction gains or losses on receivables under ASC 830. Wrong (D): Reclassification between current and long-term does not affect the exchange rate or create a transaction gain.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — computing translated retained earnings with dividends
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$A foreign subsidiary (EUR functional currency) begins the year with translated retained earnings of $2,400,000. During the year, it earns net income of EUR 800,000 (translated at the average rate of $1.08/EUR = $864,000) and declares dividends of EUR 200,000 when the spot rate is $1.05/EUR. What is the ending translated retained earnings under the current rate method?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$3,054,000", "$3,074,000", "$3,264,000", "$3,050,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the current rate method, retained earnings rolls forward: beginning balance ($2,400,000) + translated net income ($864,000) - translated dividends (EUR 200,000 x $1.05 = $210,000) = $3,054,000. Dividends are translated at the spot rate on the declaration date. Wrong (B): This likely uses an incorrect rate for dividends. Wrong (C): This fails to subtract dividends from the retained earnings calculation. Wrong (D): This uses an incorrect rate for either income or dividends.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: hard / L3 — analyzing comprehensive income differences between methods
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Vanguard Corp. (U.S.) has a foreign subsidiary that would report under the current rate method if its functional currency is LC, or the temporal method if its functional currency is USD. The subsidiary has significant nonmonetary assets and net monetary liabilities, and the local currency depreciated during the year. How does the choice of translation method affect the location and magnitude of the exchange-related charge in the parent's comprehensive income?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Both methods produce the same total comprehensive income charge, differing only in presentation between earnings and OCI", "The current rate method produces a larger OCI loss because all net assets are exposed, while the temporal method produces a smaller earnings gain from the net monetary liability position", "The temporal method produces a larger comprehensive income charge because both monetary and nonmonetary items are exposed", "The current rate method produces an OCI gain while the temporal method produces an earnings loss of the same amount"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the current rate method, all net assets are exposed to translation, producing a CTA loss in OCI when the LC depreciates. Under the temporal method, only net monetary items are exposed; with net monetary liabilities, a depreciating LC produces a remeasurement gain in earnings (liabilities cost less in dollars). The methods differ in both magnitude (net assets vs. net monetary position) and location (OCI vs. earnings). Wrong (A): Total comprehensive income generally differs between the methods because different items are exposed and nonmonetary assets are measured at different rates. Wrong (C): The temporal method excludes nonmonetary items from exchange exposure, so less — not more — is exposed. Wrong (D): Under the current rate method, a depreciating LC produces an OCI loss on net assets, not a gain.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — analyzing the effect of a mid-year subsidiary acquisition on CTA
(4, 'Foreign Currency Transactions', $EXPL$Monroe Corp. (U.S.) acquires a 100% interest in a foreign subsidiary on July 1 when the spot rate is $0.90/LC. The subsidiary's full-year net income is LC 2,000,000, earned evenly throughout the year. The average rate for July 1 to December 31 is $0.88/LC, and the year-end rate is $0.85/LC. Under ASC 830, which period's results are translated for consolidation, and what rate complexity arises for the CTA computation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The full year's income is translated at the full-year average rate since the subsidiary existed all year", "Only post-acquisition income (July 1 to December 31) is translated at the post-acquisition average rate, and the CTA reflects only post-acquisition rate differences", "The full year's income is included in consolidation but the CTA computation uses the pre-acquisition average rate for the first half", "Post-acquisition income is translated at the acquisition-date spot rate of $0.90 with no resulting CTA impact"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 830-10-45, only the post-acquisition results of a mid-year acquisition are included in the consolidated income statement. Net income of LC 1,000,000 (July 1 to December 31) is translated at the post-acquisition average rate ($0.88 = $880,000). The CTA computation reflects the mismatch between current-rate-translated balance sheet changes and average-rate-translated income only for the post-acquisition period. Wrong (A): Pre-acquisition income belongs to the former owners and is not translated for the acquirer's consolidated statements. Wrong (C): Pre-acquisition rates are irrelevant because only post-acquisition results are consolidated. Wrong (D): The average rate (not the acquisition-date spot rate) is used for income translation, and CTA arises from the rate differential.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
